## Goal
Run a principal component analysis of the gene count matrix for the rnaseq_bulk assay and plot
the samples on the first two principal components, with points coloured by condition
(untreated vs treated), to see how the six samples group relative to one another.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` (native) | `01_prepare_samplesheets` | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda environment
(pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1 — all in the lockfile),
submitted as a single batch job. Both inputs are opened read-only; nothing upstream is written.

1. Resolve both inputs with `_system/resolve_artifact.py --assay rnaseq_bulk --type counts_gene`
   and `--type design`; abort if either fails to resolve.
2. Load the count matrix (tab-separated; `gene_id` as index, `gene_name` dropped). Load the
   design; check that its `sample_id` set equals the matrix's sample columns exactly, and order
   the matrix columns by the design. Abort on any mismatch.
3. Filter: keep genes with a count of at least 10 in at least 3 samples (the size of the
   smallest condition group).
4. Normalise: DESeq2-style median-of-ratios size factors, computed in numpy on the filtered
   matrix (geometric mean per gene over genes with no zero count; size factor = median ratio
   per sample). Divide counts by the size factors, then transform with log2(x + 1).
5. Feature selection: take the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples of the
   log-transformed values, or all filtered genes if fewer than 500 pass step 3.
6. PCA: `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))` on the
   samples x genes matrix, mean-centred per gene, not scaled to unit variance (the
   convention of DESeq2's `plotPCA`).
7. Write the per-sample PC scores with `condition` and `replicate` from the design, and the
   explained-variance table.
8. Plot PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib: one point per sample, coloured by `condition` (two
   categorical colours, with a legend), each point labelled with its sample ID, axis labels
   giving the percentage of variance explained. Saved as PNG (300 dpi) and PDF.
9. Log the number of genes surviving step 3 and used in step 5, and the size factors, to stdout
   (captured in the batch job log).

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

| File | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| results/pca_scores.tsv | table | one row per sample: sample_id, condition, replicate, PC1..PCn scores |
| results/pca_variance_explained.tsv | table | one row per component: component, variance explained (fraction), cumulative fraction |
| results/pca_pc1_pc2_by_condition.png | figure | PC1 vs PC2 scatter, points coloured by condition, labelled by sample |
| results/pca_pc1_pc2_by_condition.pdf | figure | the same plot, vector format |

## Execution
Runs: batch
One job through the workspace's executor (`_system/executorlib.py submit`, Slurm here), 1 CPU,
2 GB memory, 15 min time limit. Expected wall time under 1 minute: the matrix is 200 genes x
6 samples. Expected total output size under 1 MB.
